I now have the complete picture. Here is the full code path for `POST /api/owners`, in order of execution.

# Overview / routing

There are TWO `@RestController` beans mapped to `POST /api/owners`, disambiguated by the `Idempotency-Key` header:

- `IdempotentOwnerController` — matches only when the `Idempotency-Key` header is present (more specific). It delegates to `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`.
- `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` — the header-less case, and the actual business logic. `IdempotentOwnerController` calls straight into it.

Bean Validation (`@Valid @RequestBody OwnerFieldsDto`) is declared on the generated `OwnersApi` interface method (and also explicitly on the idempotent controller). Custom `ConstraintValidator`s: none apply to owners (the only one, `PetAgeValidator`, is for pets). Entity lifecycle callbacks (`@PrePersist` etc.), entity listeners, `@EventListener`/`@TransactionalEventListener`, servlet filters, and `HandlerInterceptor`s: none exist in the project. The only `@Aspect` is `CallMonitoringAspect`, which by its own pointcut only wraps `@Repository`-annotated beans (the active Spring-Data-JPA repository is not one, so it does not join here). "Events" and "emails" in this codebase are logger emissions, not Spring events.

Active persistence profile: `spring.profiles.active=h2,spring-data-jpa` (`src/main/resources/application.properties:20`), so `saveOwner` resolves to the auto-generated Spring Data `SpringDataOwnerRepository.save`.

